# Using Alternate Javadoc Tool

By default, the Maven Javadoc Plugin runs the Javadoc tool of the JDK that runs Maven: it looks for the `javadoc`
executable under the Java property _java.home_, then under the environment variable _JAVA_HOME_. To build the
documentation with another JDK, select it with a toolchain or give the path of its `javadoc` executable.

Using a Toolchain
-----------------

The [&lt;jdkToolchain/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html${esc.hash}jdkToolchain) parameter selects a JDK from your
`~/.m2/toolchains.xml`, so the POM stays the same on every machine. It overrides a toolchain selected by the
maven-toolchains-plugin. For example, with this toolchain:

```xml
<toolchains>
  <toolchain>
    <type>jdk</type>
    <provides>
      <version>17</version>
    </provides>
    <configuration>
      <jdkHome>/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk</jdkHome>
    </configuration>
  </toolchain>
</toolchains>
```

and this configuration:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting> (or <build>)
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <jdkToolchain>
            <version>17</version>
          </jdkToolchain>
          ...
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      ...
    </plugins>
  </reporting> (or </build>)
  ...
</project>
```

the plugin logs the JDK it uses:

```text
[INFO] Toolchain in maven-javadoc-plugin: JDK[/usr/lib/jvm/java-17-openjdk]
```

See the [Guide to Using Toolchains](https://maven.apache.org/guides/mini/guide-using-toolchains.html) for more
information.

Using Alternate Javadoc Executable
----------------------------------

You can also specify the executable of the Javadoc Tool to use with the
[&lt;javadocExecutable/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html${esc.hash}javadocExecutable) parameter. The path of the JDK `bin`
directory is enough. For example:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting> (or <build>)
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <javadocExecutable>${esc.d}{env.JAVA_17_HOME}/bin/javadoc</javadocExecutable>
          ...
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      ...
    </plugins>
  </reporting> (or </build>)
  ...
</project>
```

**IMPORTANT NOTE**: a literal path in [&lt;javadocExecutable/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html${esc.hash}javadocExecutable)
makes the build depend on one machine. Use an environment variable, as above, or a POM property, or use a
toolchain instead.

Using Alternate Javadoc Version
-------------------------------

The Maven Javadoc Plugin detects the version of the Javadoc Tool by calling _javadoc -J-version_.

You can also specify the version of the Javadoc Tool executable. For example:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting> (or <build>)
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <javadocVersion>17</javadocVersion>
          ...
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      ...
    </plugins>
  </reporting> (or </build>)
  ...
</project>
```

**Note**: If the _javadocVersion_ you specify differs from the version of the Javadoc Tool executable, the plugin
logs a warning such as:

```text
[WARNING] Are you sure about the <javadocVersion/> parameter? It seems to be 21.0.13
```
